1. 2×2 System Example
 
Given the system of linear equations:

\begin{cases}
2x - y = 0 \\
-x + 2y = 3
\end{cases}

 
Matrix form
 

\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ -1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}}_{A}
\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}}_{X}
=
\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}}_{b}
\quad \Rightarrow \quad AX = b

 
Column picture
 
The system can be interpreted as a linear combination of the columns of matrix A:

x\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} + y\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}

Solution: x=1,\ y=2
Verification:

1\cdot\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} + 2\cdot\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 2-2 \\ -1+4 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}

 
Row picture
 
Each equation represents a line in the xy-plane:
 
- Line 1: 2x - y = 0
​
- Line 2: -x + 2y = 3
The solution (x,y)=(1,2) is the intersection point of the two lines.
 
 
 
2. 3×3 System Example
 
Given the system of linear equations:

\begin{cases}
2x - y = 0 \\
-x + 2y - z = -1 \\
-3y + 4z = 4
\end{cases}

 
Matrix form
 

A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 & 0 \\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\ 0 & -3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}, \quad b = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}

 
Column picture
 
Linear combination of the columns of matrix A:

x\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + y\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 2 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix} + z\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}

Solutions shown:
 
- Case 1: x=0,\ y=0,\ z=1
Verification: 0\cdot\text{col}_1 + 0\cdot\text{col}_2 + 1\cdot\text{col}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix} = b
​
- Case 2: x=1,\ y=1,\ z=0
Verification: 1\cdot\text{col}_1 + 1\cdot\text{col}_2 + 0\cdot\text{col}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 2-1 \\ -1+2 \\ 0-3 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}
 
Row picture
 
Each equation represents a plane in 3D space. The solution is the intersection point of the three planes.
 
 
 
Core Question
 
Can I solve AX = b for every b?
 
 
 
Key Terms
 
Row picture: Viewing each equation as a line or plane in coordinate space; the solution is the intersection of these lines or planes.
Column picture: Viewing the system as a linear combination of the matrix’s column vectors; solving the system means finding scalars to combine these columns into the vector b.
Matrix form: Representing the entire system of equations compactly as AX = b.
Linear combination: A sum of scalar multiples of vectors, which is the core concept behind the column picture.

