Lec 3
 
Matrix multiplication (4 ways!)
Inverse of A, AB, A^T, Gauss-Jordan / find A^{-1}
 

A_{m \times n}, \quad B_{n \times p}, \quad C = AB_{m \times p}

 
- C_{ij} = \sum_{k=1}^n a_{ik}b_{kj}
Example: C_{34} = (\text{row 3 of } A) \cdot (\text{col 4 of } B) = a_{31}b_{14} + a_{32}b_{24} + \dots + a_{3n}b_{n4}
- Column perspective: A \times \text{col}(B) = \text{col}(C)
Columns of C are combinations of columns of A.
- Row perspective: \text{row}(A) \times B = \text{row}(C)
Rows of C are combinations of rows of B.

Lec 3-2
 
 
 
Matrix Multiplication (4th way)
 
Outer product: Columns of A × Rows of B
 

(m \times 1) \times (1 \times p)

 
Example:
 

\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 6 \end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 12 \\ 3 & 18 \\ 4 & 24 \end{bmatrix}

 

\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 7 \\ 3 & 8 \\ 4 & 9 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 6 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 6 \end{bmatrix}
+
\begin{bmatrix} 7 \\ 8 \\ 9 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}

 

AB = \text{sum of (columns of } A \times \text{rows of } B)

 
 
 
Block Multiplication
 

\begin{bmatrix} A_1 & A_2 \\ A_3 & A_4 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} B_1 & B_2 \\ B_3 & B_4 \end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix} A_1B_1 + A_2B_3 & A_1B_2 + A_2B_4 \\ A_3B_1 + A_4B_3 & A_3B_2 + A_4B_4 \end{bmatrix}

 
 
 
Inverses
 
Definition: A^{-1}A = I = AA^{-1}
 
- If this matrix exists → invertible / non-singular
- Singular case (no inverse): There exists a non-zero vector x \neq 0 such that Ax = 0
Example:

A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 2 & 6 \end{bmatrix}, \quad Ax = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 2 & 6 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}

 
 
 
Gauss-Jordan Elimination (to find A^{-1})
 
Goal: Solve all equations at once.
 
- Augmented matrix: \begin{bmatrix} A & I \end{bmatrix}
- Row operations produce: \begin{bmatrix} I & A^{-1} \end{bmatrix}
- The elimination matrix E satisfies EA = I, which tells us E = A^{-1}.
 
Example:
 

\left[
\begin{array}{cc|cc}
1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\
2 & 7 & 0 & 1
\end{array}
\right]
\to
\left[
\begin{array}{cc|cc}
1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & -2 & 1
\end{array}
\right]
\to
\left[
\begin{array}{cc|cc}
1 & 0 & 7 & -3 \\
0 & 1 & -2 & 1
\end{array}
\right]

 
Result: A^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 7 & -3 \\ -2 & 1 \end{bmatrix}